# Haskell practices

Deeper notes: skill `haskell-canon` → [lessons.md](../skills/haskell-canon/lessons.md).

## Parse, don't validate

([Alexis King](https://lexi-lambda.github.io/blog/2019/11/05/parse-don-t-validate/)):
a check that returns `()` / only throws **discards the proof**. Prefer parsers
that return a refined type. Strengthen arguments (`NonEmpty`, typed morphisms)
instead of weakening every callee to `Maybe` and re-handling “impossible”
cases. Push proof upward to the boundary; no shotgun parsing (don’t act on
half-validated input). Suspect `m ()` validators whose job is erroring.

```haskell
-- BAD: validate, throw proof away, re-check downstream
assertShape v >> applyLoose v

-- GOOD: parse once; callees require the refined type
applyHeff = leftEnv .$ siteMap .$ rightEnv
```

Compose typed morphisms (`.$`, `⊗^`, `trace`, unitors, `siteDagger`). Runtime
checks belong at IO / array boundaries and in `*.Reference` — not in the core
TN path. Brand or rank-2-quantify session-like values when mixing them would be
a real bug (ad `forall s` / bound `Var` / `Scope`).

## Scheme vs algebra

For recursive structure (N-site folds, env accumulation, transfers): name the
recursion scheme once and write a non-recursive algebra. Do not hand-roll
`fmap`-style recursion at every call site (recursion-schemes lesson).

## API shape

- Prefer a small family with systematic variants over one-off names (ad-style
  suffixes / Fixed↔General extensions).
- Prefer final, composable encodings (pass capabilities / morphisms) over
  closed case-splits on representation (discrimination lesson).
- Power-to-weight: do not add heavy type machinery unless it kills a real bug
  class (bound: Derived > Overkill).
- Keep partial/unsafe helpers off the production import path (ad Internal).

## Anti-boilerplate

- Prefer a short composition that reuses existing helpers over a long
  case / list / index solution.
- If a small idea needs a lot of plumbing, **stop and redesign**.
- Do not invent parallel APIs that duplicate Fixed / General / Reference with
  slight renames.

## Type-driven process ([Haskell Guide](https://haskell-docs.netlify.app/))

- Grow features top-down with honest `undefined` stubs; let the compiler infer
  stub types before filling them in.
- Debug type errors by substituting `undefined` to localize the hole; then ask
  for its type (`cabal repl` / `:t` / HLS).
- Prefer folds / named schemes over hand-rolled recursion and imperative
  `State` loops when the problem is a fold.
- Prefer existing typeclasses + `newtype` wrappers over inventing new classes.
- Custom types enforce conceptual distinctions (`ChessSquare` ≠ `(Int,Int)`);
  keep that discipline for bonds / sites / morphisms.

## Incremental edits

- Prefer the smallest typed milestone that typechecks.
- One conceptual change per edit cycle when possible; verify with
  `cabal build quantum:lib:quantum` before stacking the next.
